<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 — the first of the shared panel parts the panel ledger asks for. `Fact` was
    hand-written six times (`TerrainModule`, `WaterModule`, `EditorWorlds`, `FontView`,
    `TextureImportView`, `CompiledSceneView`), each of them four lines of element building, and two
    of the six disagreed about where the value's text goes. This is the four lines, once.

    ⚠ **A `UiElement` rather than a `Component`, so `into.Add<FactRow>()` is the C# spelling.** The
    panels that build rows today build them into an element they are holding, and they are not
    markup yet; a `Component` would have made this reachable only from a `.vxml`, which is the
    "built but never fed" trade. `@inherits` costs nothing and lets the same type be
    `<FactRow Name="…" Value="…" />` in a `@for` the day a panel is ported.

    ⚠ **`ref` on both cells rather than an interpolation, and this is the whole reason the part had
    to exist before the ports could.** `<fact-name>@Name</fact-name>` does not set `fact-name`'s
    `Text`; it appends a `text` *child*, because a markup interpolation is `BuildContext.Text` and
    there is no other spelling. An intrinsic tag's attributes are not property assignments either —
    `BuildContext.Attribute` special-cases `class` and `style` and sends everything else to
    `StyleTree.SetAttribute`, which is a selector attribute and not `UiElement.Text`. So markup on
    its own cannot reproduce `row.Add("fact-name").Text = label`, and the row's own `ref` is the
    only escape that survives being used inside a caller's `@for` (a `ref` in the loop body itself
    is `VXML2010`).

    ⚠ **`fact-value` keeps its `text` child and `fact-name` does not, because that is what the four
    callers this replaces already do.** The asymmetry is not tidy and is deliberately preserved: a
    `text` child is a box of its own and the parent's `Text` is not, so moving either one is a
    layout change wearing a refactor's clothes.

    ⚠ **The rules are still `AssetEditorTheme`'s**, which is a layering wrinkle rather than an
    oversight: `fact-row`, `fact-name` and `fact-value` are declared there and overridden by
    `frame-editor fact-name` in the same sheet, and moving them into `EditorTheme` would move them
    up the load order and change which declaration wins. The tag names are the contract; the sheet
    that styles them is `Vixen.Editor.App`'s composition, which installs both.
-->
@component FactRow
@namespace Vixen.Editor.Ui
@inherits Vixen.Ui.UiElement
@tag fact-row
@using Vixen.Ui

<fact-name ref="@NameCell" />

<fact-value>
    <text ref="@ValueText" />
</fact-value>

@code {
    string label = string.Empty;
    string reading = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The cell holding the row's name.</summary>
    public UiElement NameCell { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The text node inside the cell holding the row's value.</summary>
    public UiElement ValueText { get; private set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>What the row is called.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Kept in a field as well as on the element</b>, because a caller may assign it before
    ///     the row is in a document. <c>Add&lt;FactRow&gt;()</c> cannot produce that order — the
    ///     build runs from <c>OnCreated</c>, which <c>UiDocument.Adopt</c> has already called by the
    ///     time <c>Add</c> returns — but <c>new FactRow()</c> can, and an assignment that threw
    ///     there would be a trap for the one caller who wrote the two lines the other way round.
    /// </remarks>
    public string Name {
        get => label;
        set {
            label = value;

            if (NameCell is not null) {
                NameCell.Text = value;
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>What it says.</summary>
    public string Value {
        get => reading;
        set {
            reading = value;

            if (ValueText is not null) {
                ValueText.Text = value;
            }
        }
    }

    partial void OnComposed() {
        NameCell.Text = label;
        ValueText.Text = reading;
    }
}
